Track symbol origin, restrict redeclaration to the declaring file, and list the symbol table

Each symbol in .axysymbols now records the source file that declared it, saved as a trailing comment (e.g. `var score = &70 ; lib.axy`). Duplicate-definition errors use it to report where the symbol was already defined: `'score' already defined at &70 (lib.axy)`.

Redeclaration is now owner-scoped: a symbol may only be redeclared by the file named as its origin. Recompiling the file that declared it stays harmless, but any other file redeclaring the name — even with an identical definition — reports an error naming the owner. Previously an identical redefinition from another file was accepted silently.

New: `axy -v` with no source file now lists the entire `.axysymbols` table instead of printing usage — variables, constants and labels, each entry naming the file that declared it. `axy -init -v` (clears then shows the report) and compiling with `-v` (current file's own symbols only) are unchanged.

- Same-file duplicate declarations still error as before (`var`, `name = value`, and labels).
- Old-format .axysymbols files without origin comments still load; the owner check applies once the file has been recompiled.
- README updated (.axysymbols section and changelog).
